You know how certain monsters (e.g. killer bee, giant bat, etc...) can
attack you from two squares away... I always thought the way they did
this is: come to the square next to you, attack you (sting, bite, etc...),
and then move back to their initial (or a different) position within the
same unit of time. The other day I was in a shop with a layout looking
like:

               --------
           ###a-S.....|    Notes: 1) shopkeeper symbolized by S for
               |@.....|    clarity. 2) shop was stock full of goodies
               |......|    (not shown).
               --------

The killer bee was two steps away, but I was wearing a ring of conflict;
I figured: the bee would step onto the door, feel the conflict and
attack the shopkeeper instead of me, and then possibly move back to where it 
was. Nope, it didn't happen that way. The darned thing still stung me.
Needless to stay, the sting was poisoned, and I lost all of my remaining
8 hit points. Oh, well. At least it was only level 5 or so. Moral of the
story: well, I can't think of any right now, but I'm sure this would make
a nice Zen koan.

By the way, I always play the priest, and as per a recent suggestion on
the newsgroup I've been playing recently without any weapons at all,
just going around kicking monsters. Try it! It's really fun, and
certainly extremely challenging.

